    "analysis": "The encryption is a stream cipher: the plaintext is XORed with a keystream\ngenerated by a **Linear Congruential Generator (LCG)**:\n\n```\nstate_{i+1} = (A * state_i + C) mod 2^32\nkeystream_i = (state_{i+1} >> 24) & 0xFF\n```\n\nwith the well-known constants `A = 1664525`, `C = 1013904223` (the classic\n*Numerical Recipes* LCG). The constants and the recurrence are public\ninformation, so none of that is secret. The only \"secret\" is the seed used\nto initialize `state`.\n\n### The bug\n\nLook closely at this line:\n\n```python\nstate = seed & 0xFFFFF\n```\n\nThe seed is masked with `0xFFFFF` (5 hex digits = **20 bits**), even though\n`state` itself is a full 32-bit value. This means no matter what\n`facility_seed` was actually chosen, only\n\n```\n2^20 = 1,048,576\n```\n\ndistinct starting states are possible. That is a *tiny* keyspace — fully\nbrute-forceable in well under a second on a laptop.\n\n### Attack plan\n\n1. Recreate the `keystream()` function locally (constants are known/public).\n2. Iterate `seed` from `0` to `2^20 - 1`.\n3. For each candidate seed, generate a keystream of the same length as the\n   ciphertext and XOR it with the ciphertext.\n4. Check whether the resulting plaintext contains the known flag prefix\n   `grodno` (or is otherwise printable/sensible text).\n5. Once found, print the plaintext and extract the flag.\n\nBecause XOR is its own inverse, decryption uses the exact same function as\nencryption:\n\n```\nplaintext = ciphertext XOR keystream(seed, len(ciphertext))\n```",
